{"st":"VA","name":"Virginia","seats":[{"census":1789,"seats":10},{"census":1790,"seats":19},{"census":1800,"seats":22},{"census":1810,"seats":23},{"census":1820,"seats":22},{"census":1830,"seats":21},{"census":1840,"seats":15},{"census":1850,"seats":13},{"census":1860,"seats":11},{"census":1870,"seats":9},{"census":1880,"seats":10},{"census":1890,"seats":10},{"census":1900,"seats":10},{"census":1910,"seats":10},{"census":1920,"seats":10},{"census":1930,"seats":9},{"census":1940,"seats":9},{"census":1950,"seats":10},{"census":1960,"seats":10},{"census":1970,"seats":10},{"census":1980,"seats":10},{"census":1990,"seats":11},{"census":2000,"seats":11},{"census":2010,"seats":11},{"census":2020,"seats":11}],"events":[{"congress":1,"year":1788,"kind":"statehood","title":"Ratified the Constitution","text":"Virginia ratified the Constitution on June 25, 1788, the 10th state to do so. The Constitution gave it 10 seats in the House until the first census.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":3,"year":1792,"kind":"census","title":"1790 census apportionment","text":"After the 1790 census, Virginia's House delegation grew from 10 to 19 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":8,"year":1802,"kind":"census","title":"1800 census apportionment","text":"After the 1800 census, Virginia's House delegation grew from 19 to 22 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":13,"year":1812,"kind":"census","title":"1810 census apportionment","text":"After the 1810 census, Virginia's House delegation grew from 22 to 23 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 23 to 22 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 22 to 21 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 21 to 15 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 15 to 13 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 13 to 11 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 11 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Virginia's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 48th Congress (1883–1885), Virginia elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Virginia kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Virginia kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Virginia kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Virginia's House delegation shrank from 10 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"at-large","title":"Statewide (general ticket) elections","text":"In the 73rd Congress (1933–1935), Virginia elected all of its representatives statewide on a general ticket instead of by district. The 1929 apportionment law had no districting rules, so states without a new map after losing or gaining seats could elect members at-large.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/","https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IF12567.html"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Virginia kept 9 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Virginia's House delegation grew from 9 to 10 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Virginia kept 10 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Virginia kept 10 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Virginia kept 10 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Virginia's House delegation grew from 10 to 11 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Virginia kept 11 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Virginia kept 11 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":115,"year":2016,"kind":"court","title":"Court-drawn map","text":"A federal court found the 3rd District was a racial gerrymander and adopted its own map in January 2016, used from that year's election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/virginia/","https://thearp.org/litigation/personhuballah-v-alcorn"]},{"congress":118,"year":2021,"kind":"court","title":"Commission deadlock; court draws map","text":"A 2020 constitutional amendment created the Virginia Redistricting Commission. When it could not agree on a congressional map, the Supreme Court of Virginia adopted one drawn by special masters in December 2021.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/virginia/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Virginia kept 11 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":119,"year":2026,"kind":"court","title":"Redistricting amendment voided","text":"Voters approved a constitutional amendment in April 2026 that would have allowed a mid-decade congressional redraw. In May 2026 the Supreme Court of Virginia ruled the result void because the legislature's first vote came after early voting had begun, and the existing map was used in 2026.","sources":["https://news.ballotpedia.org/2026/05/08/virginia-supreme-court-voids-election-results-for-virginia-redistricting-referendum-blocking-implementation-of-a-map-that-would-have-shifted-four-congressional-seats-towards-democrats/"]}],"compactness":{"c001":{"VA-1":0.221,"VA-2":0.297,"VA-3":0.297,"VA-4":0.273,"VA-5":0.303,"VA-6":0.325,"VA-7":0.105,"VA-8":0.045,"VA-9":0.297,"VA-10":0.169},"c002":{"VA-1":0.221,"VA-2":0.297,"VA-3":0.297,"VA-4":0.273,"VA-5":0.303,"VA-6":0.325,"VA-7":0.105,"VA-8":0.045,"VA-9":0.297,"VA-10":0.169},"c003":{"VA-1":0.417,"VA-2":0.288,"VA-3":0.245,"VA-4":0.338,"VA-5":0.259,"VA-6":0.447,"VA-7":0.301,"VA-8":0.457,"VA-9":0.513,"VA-10":0.47,"VA-11":0.175,"VA-12":0.026,"VA-13":0.185,"VA-14":0.259,"VA-15":0.389,"VA-16":0.245,"VA-17":0.376,"VA-18":0.384,"VA-19":0.157},"c004":{"VA-1":0.417,"VA-2":0.288,"VA-3":0.245,"VA-4":0.338,"VA-5":0.259,"VA-6":0.447,"VA-7":0.301,"VA-8":0.457,"VA-9":0.513,"VA-10":0.47,"VA-11":0.175,"VA-12":0.026,"VA-13":0.185,"VA-14":0.259,"VA-15":0.389,"VA-16":0.245,"VA-17":0.376,"VA-18":0.384,"VA-19":0.157},"c005":{"VA-1":0.417,"VA-2":0.288,"VA-3":0.245,"VA-4":0.338,"VA-5":0.259,"VA-6":0.447,"VA-7":0.301,"VA-8":0.457,"VA-9":0.513,"VA-10":0.47,"VA-11":0.175,"VA-12":0.026,"VA-13":0.185,"VA-14":0.259,"VA-15":0.389,"VA-16":0.245,"VA-17":0.376,"VA-18":0.384,"VA-19":0.157},"c006":{"VA-1":0.417,"VA-2":0.288,"VA-3":0.245,"VA-4":0.338,"VA-5":0.259,"VA-6":0.447,"VA-7":0.301,"VA-8":0.457,"VA-9":0.513,"VA-10":0.47,"VA-11":0.175,"VA-12":0.026,"VA-13":0.185,"VA-14":0.259,"VA-15":0.389,"VA-16":0.245,"VA-17":0.376,"VA-18":0.384,"VA-19":0.157},"c007":{"VA-1":0.417,"VA-2":0.288,"VA-3":0.245,"VA-4":0.338,"VA-5":0.259,"VA-6":0.447,"VA-7":0.301,"VA-8":0.457,"VA-9":0.513,"VA-10":0.47,"VA-11":0.175,"VA-12":0.026,"VA-13":0.185,"VA-14":0.259,"VA-15":0.389,"VA-16":0.245,"VA-17":0.376,"VA-18":0.384,"VA-19":0.157},"c008":{"VA-1":0.344,"VA-2":0.23,"VA-3":0.475,"VA-4":0.33,"VA-5":0.231,"VA-6":0.362,"VA-7":0.376,"VA-8":0.1