It was first used in the 1792 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":19},"c008":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1800 census, which raised Virginia's seats from 19 to 22. It was first used in the 1802 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":22},"c010":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in 3 districts from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c013":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1810 census, which raised Virginia's seats from 22 to 23. It was first used in the 1812 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":22},"c018":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1820 census, which cut Virginia's seats from 23 to 22. It was first used in the 1822 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":22},"c023":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1830 census, which cut Virginia's seats from 22 to 21. It was first used in the 1832 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":17},"c025":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1836 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c026":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in 2 districts from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c028":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1840 census, which cut Virginia's seats from 21 to 15. It was first used in the 1842 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":21},"c030":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1846 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c032":{"why":"small","kind":"other","text":"Not a new map: the historical boundary records show only slight differences in 2 districts from the previous Congress, and no statewide redistricting is recorded for this election.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c033":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1850 census, which cut Virginia's seats from 15 to 13. It was first used in the 1852 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":15},"c036":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1858 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c037":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1860 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":11},"c038":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1860 census, which cut Virginia's seats from 13 to 11. It was first used in the 1862 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c039":{"why":"at-large","kind":"other","text":"Virginia sent no members to the 39th Congress (1865–1867), so the map shows only its outline and no districts.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":0},"c040":{"why":"at-large","kind":"other","text":"From the 40th Congress the historical boundary records again show districts for Virginia, after Congresses in which it sent no members.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":0},"c041":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1868 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c043":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1870 census, which cut Virginia's seats from 11 to 9. It was first used in the 1872 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c048":{"why":"at-large","kind":"at-large","text":"After the 1880 census raised Virginia's seats from 9 to 10, the state kept its existing districts and elected one member statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":0},"c049":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1884 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":10},"c053":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1890 census, which left Virginia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1892 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":2},"c061":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1908 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c063":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1910 census, which left Virginia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1912 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":3},"c073":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"After the 1930 census changed Virginia's seats from 10 to 9, Virginia still elected all of its members statewide, so it had no districts to redraw.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":10},"c074":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1934 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c083":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1950 census, which raised Virginia's seats from 9 to 10. It was first used in the 1952 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":7},"c090":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1966 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":10},"c093":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1970 census, which left Virginia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1972 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":10},"c098":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1980 census, which left Virginia with 10 seats. It was first used in the 1982 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":9},"c103":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1990 census, which raised Virginia's seats from 10 to 11. It was first used in the 1992 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":11},"c106":{"why":"unexplained","kind":"other","text":"Virginia's district lines were redrawn between censuses, first used in the 1998 election. The reason has not been researched yet.","sources":["https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":5},"c108":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2000 census, which left Virginia with 11 seats. It was first used in the 2002 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":11},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which left Virginia with 11 seats. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":11},"c115":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Court-drawn map. A federal court found the 3rd District was a racial gerrymander and adopted its own map in January 2016, used from that year's election.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/virginia/","https://thearp.org/litigation/personhuballah-v-alcorn"],"districtsChanged":7},"c118":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Commission deadlock; court draws map. A 2020 constitutional amendment created the Virginia Redistricting Commission. When it could not agree on a congressional map, the Supreme Court of Virginia adopted one drawn by special masters in December 2021.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/virginia/"],"districtsChanged":11}}}